Here are the three TUnit skip patterns:

**1. Skip a single test method**

```csharp
[Test]
[Skip("Waiting on payment-gateway sandbox provisioning")]
public async Task ChargeCard_ReturnsSuccess()
{
    // …
}
```

**2. Skip every test in an assembly (conditionally)**

```csharp
// In any file (e.g. AssemblyAttributes.cs)
#if SMOKE
[assembly: Skip("Assembly-level skip: smoke configuration — full suite disabled")]
#endif
```

Build with `-p:DefineConstants=SMOKE` to activate.

**3. Runtime / programmatic skip inside a test**

```csharp
[Test]
public async Task SyncAdData_RequiresVpn()
{
    if (!IsOnCorporateVpn())
    {
        Assert.Skip("Machine is not on the corporate VPN — skipping");
    }

    // … rest of test
}
```

`Assert.Skip()` throws a special exception that TUnit catches, marking the test as **skipped** (not failed) in the results.

**Summary**

| Scenario | Mechanism | Granularity |
|---|---|---|
| Known blocker | `[Skip("reason")]` on method/class | Compile-time, single test or class |
| Entire assembly | `[assembly: Skip("reason")]` | Compile-time, all tests |
| Runtime decision | `Assert.Skip("reason")` | Inside test body, conditional |
